## Problem
Governance and security teams are expected to stay current on AI risk — new regulations, fresh vulnerabilities, and real-world incidents. Still, the sources are scattered across standards bodies, vendor blogs, and incident databases. Manually checking them is a chore that quietly slips. The tempting fix is full automation: have an LLM read the news and write the brief. But an unreviewed AI summary of security news is exactly the ungoverned-AI pattern this kind of work exists to prevent.

## Approach
- A local Python pipeline (feedparser + SQLite, scheduled with macOS launchd) that collects AI-security news from multiple RSS feeds, filters it to relevant items by keyword, deduplicates against a local database, and writes a clean digest.
- A deliberate **human-in-the-loop review gate**: automation ends at the digest; a person turns it into the final brief, correcting mis-tags and cutting low-signal items before anything is published.
- A saved, versioned prompt that turns each digest into a structured brief, with every item mapped to an OWASP LLM Top 10 category, NIST AI RMF function, or EU AI Act provision.
- Runs entirely on one machine — no third-party automation cloud, no data leaving the boundary, and no API key in the pipeline.

