## Reflection
The most valuable decision in this project was the one to build *less* automation. It would have been faster to let a model write and send the brief unattended. Still, LLM summaries of security news mis-tag severity and hallucinate framework mappings, and publishing that unreviewed would undercut the entire premise of the work. Scoping the machine to collection and filtering, and keeping judgment with a human, is a smaller claim and a more defensible one. Building it in Python rather than a no-code tool kept all data local. It produced a readable artifact, and persistent deduplication via SQLite gives the monitor a memory across weeks, not just a per-run filter. In v2, I want to move from monitoring to scoring — ranking each item against a defined risk register while keeping the human gate intact.
